  "response": "The collaborative robot struck a worker's hand during a pick-and-place cycle, and that injury led the occupational safety regulator to open an investigation. Investigators retrieved the robot controller's event log covering the seconds preceding the contact. That log is the central artefact of such an inquiry, since it records commanded velocities, sensed joint torques, parameter checksums and the state of each safety function at millisecond resolution.\n\nThe allocation of responsibility is sharply contested. The manufacturer attributed the incident to the factory's modification of the robot's speed parameters, whereas the factory attributed the incident to a defect in the force-limiting firmware. Adjudicating between those accounts requires evidence that discriminates the layer in which contact energy is sensed from the layer in which the resulting limit is enforced.\n\nThe quasi-static contact force limit of 140 N for the hand and finger region issues from the fact that ISO/TS 15066 specifies biomechanical force and pressure limits for collaborative robot operation. The same specification permits transient contact forces of up to 600 N at the hand, a tolerance that cannot be squared with the 140 N quasi-static ceiling for that same anatomical region, since both figures cannot govern one region at once. Those codified thresholds are what led investigators to reproduce the contact using an instrumented force-and-pressure measuring device, mounted on a fixture that replicated the worker's hand position and the closing geometry of the gripper. The reproduction shows that the measured peak contact force exceeded the applicable biomechanical limit for that body region.\n\nAn exceedance of that kind establishes that the protective function failed, but not yet where. Interrogation of the calibration record and of the configured safety parameters narrows the question to two findings, and at least one of them obtained, perhaps both: the torque sensor's output had drifted beyond its specified tolerance, and the control software's collision-detection threshold was configured above the applicable biomechanical limit. Power and force limiting in collaborative robots is implemented solely in mechanical hardware, a position that cannot stand together with a detection threshold residing in the control software as a configurable value.\n\nThe discrimination therefore rests on two independent checks: bench calibration of the removed torque sensor against a traceable reference, which fixes drift without reference to any software setting, and byte-level comparison of the deployed parameter set and firmware signature against the versions released by the manufacturer, which fixes who last altered the enforced limit. Where the sensor is found within tolerance and the threshold altered after commissioning, the deploying factory bears the defect; where the signed firmware is unmodified and the sensor nonetheless drifted undetected by its own diagnostic routine, the fault lies upstream in the product as supplied.",
